// Fixture: init_nested_uninitialized_dotted_path
// Rule under test: GDDL Spec v4 §7, §12 phase 8 -- extends the group's existing
// single-fixture coverage (init_uninitialized_only_caught_at_export.gddl, which only
// ever tests a TOP-LEVEL uninitialized field) to a genuinely nested case. Confirms
// phase 8 reports the full dotted path to the missing field (e.g. `object.inner.value`)
// at real nesting depth, not just a bare top-level field name -- a compiler that only
// tracks "which top-level field is incomplete" without threading the path through
// nested struct resolution could plausibly report something less specific (e.g. just
// `object`, or just `value` with no path context) instead of the precise dotted
// location.

define Inner
	value = u32

define Object
	inner = Inner

define Container
	object = Object

Container Holder
	object
		inner
			// value: never touched anywhere. Container is not `delete`, so this must
			// fail at phase 8 (Container has nothing else to set -- `object` is its
			// only field, and `object.inner.value` is the only leaf ever reachable).

// UPDATE (new concept, not known when this fixture was written): GDDL Spec v4 §12.1
// defines warnings -- non-blocking diagnostics distinct from errors. The first defined
// warning is exactly what `inner` below does: a bare struct-field entry with zero
// children (§6.4). This fixture is expected to be the first one in the corpus carrying
// a warning alongside its phase-8 error, once re-captured. Not restructuring the
// "Expected" comment below to guess at the warning's exact reported shape -- see
// GOLDEN_FORMAT.md's "Pending schema extension" note for why.

// Expected (prediction, pending golden output from Compiler Core reference run):
//   Compile error / incomplete against Holder: missing field reported as the full
//   dotted path `object.inner.value`, not just `value` or just `object`. Phase 8
//   (Final Validation), matching the established pattern from
//   init_uninitialized_only_caught_at_export.gddl.
//   Also expected (see note above): a warning against `inner` for being a bare
//   struct-field entry with zero children -- exact reported shape not predicted here.
